op or H top to  figure out what exactly a 10 or 20,000  line program was doing you probably  don't that's the whole reason that flame  graphs exist in fact but let's suppose  now instead of running that 10,000 line  file you're analyzing a hundred line  file that's running as part of its own  process in a docker container what you  can see here is its immediately evident  that this service has a memory leak and  it has a go routine leak but it's very  clear to figure out exactly what's going  on here because there's only a hundred  lines in the file since the service  itself is so small we can forget about  it most of the time and then when it's  having issues we can pinpoint exactly  where problems are occurring  which is what the nice thing that  microservices have given us additionally  we split these micro services and what  we call different clusters so each  cluster is separated by concerns so in  addition to monitoring at the service  level we get a little more insight into  what particular hosts are doing of  course I couldn't talk only about the  clusters without explaining what exactly  the services look like so this is a  typical segment service and I'll explain  each of these in non AWS terms as well  route 53 is a waw SS dns service this is  indicating which load balancer we should  point something at say if we connect to  auth service segment local will register  a DNS entry for it additionally every  service will have its own load balancer  and that load balancer will point to  particular instances or containers on  that are running on those instances that  register themselves with the load  balancer what this means is that we  don't need any form of service discovery  there's nothing like console or at CD  running here and admittedly there's some  performance overhead from that and it  doesn't work in all cases say if you  have a driver which actually needs to  connect to multiple instances for  redundancy or failover but it does work  fairly well for most of our apps I can't  be out this that this is only this will  only work well for you if microservices  are actually easy to boot right  otherwise now you have two problems you  have a bunch of services and it's really  hard to orchestrate and run them do this  we use terraform for those of you who  haven't used terraform it's pretty  similar to amazon's cloud formation  service it basically describes your  infrastructure in the form of JSON or  terra forms HCL I guess hachey corp  configuration language but basically  what this does is it provides minimal  work for a programmer who's trying to  create their own micro service in their  case they just say which cluster they  want it to run on the name and version  of the docker container and then give it  some memory and CPU and boom we're off  to the races in terms of actually  creating this thing and running it in  production additionally we built  significant tooling around actually  deploy  knowing these so that deploying them is  easy fast and safe whenever you get  pushed to a repo github will trigger a  build in circle our CI tool and from  there it will actually build a docker  image whenever the CI tool passes from  there we can deploy be a slack or via  CLI tool which updates the image that's  running in ecs amazon scheduler and then  it pulls new versions of the code down  from docker hub in this way it makes  running new containers very easy and  safe it means that we can roll back  pretty much instantly since all the old  versions are also tagged in docker hub  additionally it means that we can run  the same versions in stage as well as  production lastly I'd like to talk about  monitoring how we ensure that the system  continues to run in production now I  like to say that the more surface area  you have the more visibility that you  need into that system in our case it's  really true so since we have 150  different external touch points which  could possibly fail yet fail at any time  we require a lot of visibility and  really invest in this religiously in  terms of the metrics that we record and  so we keep track for every integration  delivery rate success rate of that  partner API end-to-end latency and any  top errors that we see coming in and  like I said we have a lot of these  metrics here but giving us splitting  each of them out into their own category  gives us the ability to really figure  out where problems are happening and  find them quickly additionally we keep a  roll up of these so we can see for a  particular integration or for the system  at large exactly what's happening with  any key area and we built our own  service toolkit to automatically record  most of these metrics they could be  things like the duration that it takes  to process a message the CPU time the  number of go routines the memory all of  these things should be things that the  programmer gets out of the box when  they're building a service from day one  so as far as the big lessons for scaling  our data pipeline which hopefully you  can use for your own internal data  pipeline or if you're building your own  ETL platform if you control the clients  you should try and make the edge as  stateless as possible this is something  that honestly I hadn't considered was a  good idea for the longest time I was  wondered about why Google Analytics or  these other large tail providers so  basically 200 on everything until I got  to the realization that none of them  care about supporting third-party  clients really for then they have a  single client and their number one  concern is preventing data loss so if  your system operates under similar  constraints I'd recommend make the edge  stateless make it just a bin for  incoming data and build clients around  that so that people can easily send data  to it the second one is that ques not  only defined service boundaries where  places can fail but they also define  scheduling and rate limiting so it's  worth thinking about when building your  queuing topology do I want everything in  this queue to have the same priority or  do I want to actually split it out and  to possibly different workers or  different topics third you want to have  micro services and workers if they can  provide great visibility and scalability  only so long as they are easy to boot it  requires a significant investment on the  infrastructure side but it's paid off  immensely for us in terms of providing  better visibility into what the running  system is doing and finally the bigger  your surface area the more visibility  and metrics you need to provide this is  something that we've learned the hard  way by not having good visibility into  the system and anywhere you can't ask is  the system working what's going wrong  right now how many messages in my  delivering is a case where you should  record stats and metrics I'd like to end  its talk with a few open questions about  what's next and what things were  considering since this system itself is  far from complete the first one is just  finding more search of fairness and what  do I mean by that well right now where  we operate with  at the integration level right messages  come in they get grouped into different  integrations or third-party services and  from there none of those will affect  each other we can spin up different  workers and essentially that system is  fair where we don't operate with  complete fairness comes down to how  customers are sending data let's say a  really large customers start sending a  giant batch of data it could mean that a  smaller customer actually has to wait  for their data to come through at all so  we're considering various options there  around either encoding the jobs within  the queue format or coming up with some  sort of internal scheduling system built  into the queue itself that would provide  good fairness on keys passing through  the queue the second idea that we kicked  around is moving over to Kafka like I  said n SQ has