ur fallback for a failure  at that level I understand you have  you're queuing with the the NS q but  what if I don't know the data center  gets hit by a natural disaster or  something yeah so currently all the NS q  nodes are backed by EBS which provides  pretty good guarantees in the case of  failure since that's all stateless what  we could do say if an entire region gets  Newt is then we can move to another  region pretty easily because we have all  that code we have the instances and we  can spin up the infrastructure basically  at the drop of the hat for then it's  still persisted yes yes oh sorry yes it  is stateful but only local to the  machine yo le  how's using docker hub in production bin  for you guys we use them as well and  they go down all the time yeah we also  have that problem is the short answer  and eventually we plan to move to the  Amazon container registry which should  provide a lot better like both security  guarantees and reliability because we  have had problems as well pushing and  pulling to them and for the most part it  ends up not being that big of a deal but  yeah occasionally when our developers  try to push a build then we run into  issues what else  yeah you didn't really talk about the  redshift integration that you guys have  I just wanted to expand on it a little  bit yes and so for those of you who are  unfamiliar recently we added support for  warehouses to our platform and what  happens here is well basically bunch up  all this data on an hourly basis that is  where the archiver comes in it will  upload to s3 unbend by each particular  project that is for and then from there  we'll run an ETL step where we actually  load it into the redshift cluster so I  didn't talk about that as much since  it's a relatively recent and B and we  don't have quite the volume of traffic  going through there since it's running  on an hourly basis and per each customer  it's only dealing with their redshift  instance for some of these partner 